# Enforce CRLF line endings on disk for every text file, on every
# platform, regardless of each contributor's `core.autocrlf` setting.
#
# How this works:
#   * Git stores all text in the index normalized to LF.
#   * On checkout, `eol=crlf` converts each text file to CRLF on disk.
#   * On commit, the same rule converts CRLF back to LF in the index.
# So the working tree is always CRLF and the repository is always LF —
# no contributor config knob can override these rules.
#
# After cloning (or after this file is added/changed), contributors
# whose working tree was already populated should run:
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git commit -m "Renormalize line endings"
# to bring existing checkouts in line with these rules.

# Default for everything Git auto-detects as text.
*           text=auto eol=crlf

# Belt-and-suspenders: explicitly mark every text file type used by
# this addon, so the eol rule applies even if Git mis-detects content.
*.lua       text eol=crlf
*.toc       text eol=crlf
*.xml       text eol=crlf
*.md        text eol=crlf
*.txt       text eol=crlf
*.json      text eol=crlf
*.yml       text eol=crlf
*.yaml      text eol=crlf
LICENSE     text eol=crlf
.gitignore  text eol=crlf

# The repo-wide `eol=crlf` above applies to `.md` too, including the generated
# `docs/test-cases.md` — and that file needs no exemption and no pipeline. The
# shared test kit's `--list` renderer writes CRLF itself, precisely because
# these repos pin `*.md text eol=crlf`, a plain redirect writes LF, and a
# regeneration command with a pipeline in it is one somebody eventually runs
# without the pipeline. So both of these are CRLF and the check is silent when
# in sync:
#   lua tests/run.lua --list > docs/test-cases.md
#   diff <(lua tests/run.lua --list) docs/test-cases.md
# NOT `git diff --exit-code -- docs/test-cases.md`: that compares against HEAD
# rather than against regenerated output, so a correctly-updated inventory
# reports as drift every time. See docs/testing.md ("Keeping the inventory &
# badge in sync").

# Binary assets — never normalize. Covers WoW addon media (textures,
# sounds) and general repo binaries.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.tga       binary
*.blp       binary
*.mp3       binary
*.ogg       binary
*.wav       binary
*.zip       binary
*.tar       binary
*.gz        binary
